url: "https://laurelpoetry.com"
abstract: >-
  Laurel is a corpus of public domain English poetry, encoded in TEI P5, in which every
  line has been scanned. It is the material underneath laurelpoetry.com, a free reading
  room, and is published separately so that the work behind the site can be examined,
  corrected and put to other uses. It grows with each release, so the counts of works,
  poems and lines are printed by the build script in the repository rather than repeated
  here.


  Every line is recorded twice: once as the meter asks for it, and once as the words
  themselves require. In Shakespeare's "Making a famine where abundance lies", the meter
  wants the line to open unstressed and the word refuses, since "Making" is MAK-ing and no
  reader would say ma-KING. That disagreement is the point of the corpus. It is where a
  poet departs from the measure, and that is where most of the interest in meter lies; a
  corpus holding only one of the two rows cannot show it. Syllables that neither settles
  are marked open rather than guessed at. Stanzas carry a rhyme scheme lettered from rhyme
  keys, and every poem has a canonical reference on the CTS model that names its edition,
  since a citation to a poem is not reproducible unless it says which text it means.


  How well the scansion works is measured rather than claimed. Against 3,183 lines marked
  by hand by prosodists, on the half of that data held back from every design decision, it
  agrees with the annotator on 92.8 per cent of syllables. Counted a line at a time, with
  a single wrong mark condemning the line, 74.5 per cent come out right, and 70 per cent
  if the lines it could not scan at all are counted as failures too. That second figure is
  the one that matters for analysis rather than display, and it is the one usually left
  out.


  The texts are in the public domain. The encoding, the scansion and the annotation are CC
  BY-SA 4.0. METHOD.md in the repository documents the method and the places where it is
  weak, including the limits of the selection and, in this release, two verse dramas whose
  division into sections is wrong.


